Executive Summary
The Pygmalion effect is a phenomenon where simply by raising expectations of someone, that same person performs at a higher level than previously. The myth of Pygmalion is an age old story that has inspired many ideas, such as the play“My Fair Lady” and the movie “The Mannequin”. Pygmalion was a sculptor who created a statue named Galatea which he fell in love with. He believed in this statue as being so real, as the story goes, that Galatea came to life. This illustrates how expecting something to be realized can actually have power to make that expectation a reality. Much has been said and studied on the Pygmalion effect, however very little of the research has been done in a business setting. This report sets out to show how trust is elemental in the realization and reliance on the Pygmalion effect being used in today’s business world. Through literature review and a specially designed and administered survey, there appears to be a clear correlation between trust and the Pygmalion effect. There seems to be many suggested correlations from the survey data of which some conclusions may be made. Not only are there correlations between trust and the meeting of expectations but also there are clear differences in these correlations in regards to gender. Although this survey is limited to only one group of seventy people working at one company, the data appears to be broad and unbiased enough for some reasonable inferences to be made.
