Introduction
This report was prepared to provide to readers something that could be useful in the management field. Its purpose is to find correlations between trust and expectations that would enable a supervisor to have a positive effect on subordinates through the usage of the Pygmalion Effect. This is the effect where by raised expectations from one’s supervisor, their performance is increased. These phenomena are very interesting and with children especially, it seems to be extremely powerful. Part of the reason Pygmalion is so effective with children is that they have so much trust in adults. This report has set out to answer these questions:
•	What kind of trust is important for Pygmalion to be effective?
•	What types of relationships can foster a Pygmalion Relationship?
•	How does the way one sees his/her supervisor affect Pygmalion relationships?
•	What effects do self-efficacy and self-expectation have on the desire to meet a supervisor’s expectation?
•	Does the gender of the supervisor and/or subordinate have any effect on the Pygmalion relationship?
To attain the desired data a survey has been built, administered and analyzed which was successfully designed to attain data that may help to answer these questions. Seventy out of four-hundred people offered the survey responded. All of the respondents were employed professionals at CH2M. Analysis of this data is the foundation of the findings and conclusions in regards to the Pygmalion Effect in the professional workplace.

Project Overview
This project was spurred from a desire to bring to the future a tool of which few possess; to share insight on the relationships between supervisor and subordinate and find ways to improve that relationship whilst using it to the company’s advantage by raising individual subordinate performance. The Pygmalion Effect is a documented occurrencethat has been under-utilized and minimally studied in the business sector. This report aims to show concrete factors which if emphasized and implemented, can lead to the successful meeting of higher supervisor expectations.  
Literature Review
The literature being reviewed has been chosen to support the Pygmalion effect and its foundation. There is a substantial literature that discussed the Pygmalion Effect however most of it is in the student/teacher or military setting.This review focuses on commonalities between studies that will contrast and support my own findings through the survey I administered. This project will be to establish how to help managers determine if, when and how they can use this phenomenon and then for them to find effective ways to use it. What has been found through this research is somewhat enlightening; not only in the support of common beliefs about Pygmalion, but also in making clear the research that has not been done. There are many studies on the Pygmalion effect and the majority of them have been done either in the educational setting or the military setting. Not many studies have been done in the business setting which is for where this project is designed.
What has been established about the Pygmalion effect is that first of all, the effect is going to be dependent on the relationship of the subordinate and the supervisor. The business setting administers a very different Pygmalion than that of an education or military setting. In the business world or workplace, there is not much real power given to the supervisor, as the subordinate gets to choose where and whom they work for. In a military setting, the supervisor has much more power and thus the relationship is different. Of the educational studies found, most of them were done on children, and again this is very different from the business setting relationship.
What the research from this literature review is geared towards is to find what elements make the Pygmalion effect happen in a business setting. It seems that trust between supervisor and subordinate is the key element in making Pygmalion effective.
	The literature reviewed here has been used to answer these questions:
•	How is Pygmalion trust built?
•	How does a supervisor know that this trust is built?
•	What types of trust are there and which ones are needed for an effective Pygmalion relationship? 

In their meta-analysis, Kierein and Gold (2000)revealed a lot about Pygmalion studies. The correlation between the Galatea effect and the Pygmalion effect are very apparent. The Galatea effect is when the subordinates’ expectations about themselves are raised and this results in a higher performance.  “Raised subordinate expectations may mediate the relationship between raised leader expectations and improved subordinate performance. Thus, the Galatea effect may be part of the Pygmalion effect” (Kierein & Gold, 2000). This article supports the idea that the makings of the Pygmalion effect are tied to what the subordinate believes. The Galatea effect is an integral part of the Pygmalion process. This is where the subordinate also believes he or she can achieve higher goals. Often this is facilitated by the supervisor.  When supervisors raise the expected level of performance from specific subordinates, they are also better leaders to the subordinates. Kierein and Gold (2000) wrote, “Through this special treatment, subordinates then develop higher self-expectations. Much of the effect of expectation-raising on performance operates through subordinates' own self-expectations. This suggests that subordinates' own self-expectations are a strong mediating variable in the Pygmalion process“(Kierein & Gold, 2000).This is leading to the issue of the relationship that has been built between the supervisor and subordinate. A subordinate must respect and trust the supervisor’s analysis of the employee in order for the employee to glean belief in the goal being accomplished. The supervisor has earned this from the subordinate most likely because he or she has the ability to do the task, and to assess the subordinate’s abilities and the task itself. What builds this trust is somewhat difficult to define but the definition is inconsequential, as all relationships will differ depending on the personality traits of the persons involved. 
Self-efficacy is a common term found in Pygmalion literature, and simply means one’s belief in his or her capacity to execute a task. This is similar to the Galatea effect but somewhat more personal. Raising one’s own expectations (Galatea) is an ultimate goal in attempting to attain Pygmalion, but actually believing that it can be done is a separate and powerful element. The little engine that could is an example of how this works. White and Locke (2000) used the example of telling a subordinate, “I know you are good” and said that is a way that Pygmalion could occur (White & Locke, 2000). The importance of trust in the relationship is seen in this example. If people are told that they can accomplish something, then they are more likely to believe it themselves. This special treatment is sometimes called “differential treatment” and which is Jussim’s (1996) second stage in his Self-fulfilling prophesy model (Jussim, 1986). Trust is built by the supervisor’s actions in general and in the way he or she treats employees. If supervisors exhibit a negative attitude or voice that they do not believe in the employee this can have negative effects. Although some subordinates may take it as a challenge, many will resent the boss and only be raising performance to prove them wrong rather than to please him or her. More often than not, if employees are told that they are not capable of doing better, then they will not perform. This is called the Golem effect. The Golem effect is the negative corollary of Pygmalion (Colman, 2015) and it shows the importance of a positive trust being built to attain Pygmalion.
Jussim’s third stage is where the subordinate reacts to the differential treatment and this involves changes in the subordinate’s values and self –esteem (Kierein & Gold, 2000). A study on the role of a leader’s behaviors in Pygmalion by Bezuijen, Berg, Dam and Thierry (2009) revealed five potential mediators in the Pygmalion relationship. The leader-member exchange relationship is the first one of the five. Bezuijen, Berg, Dam & Thierry (2009) stated that “leaders invest valuable resources in high leader-member exchange (LMX) relationships, with positive consequences for the employees’ development. In time employees become trusted assistants and reciprocate.” The type of leader is critical in the LMX as Howard, Tang and Austin (2014) also expressed that the relationship between moral leadership and employee creativity is mediated by the employee identification with the leader and LMX (Howard, Tang, & Austin, 2014). “Mediated” means exhibiting indirect causation, connection, or relation which means that trust is an elementary element. How an employee “identifies” with the supervisor, is most importantly founded in the level of trust that has been earned by that supervisor.
The LMX relationship melds with Pygmalion theory because it reflects the importance of the feelings supervisor and subordinate have towards each other. After all, the feelings of love towards Galatea that were experienced by Pygmalion himself, is what was said to have led to her becoming real.Graen and Uhl-Bien (1995) found that “a warm social emotional climate resembles a high-quality LMX relationship; for example, a relationship of trust, respect, and obligation between leader and employee” (Graen & Uhl-Bien, 1995,pp. 219-247). This implies that good leaders that would like to use the Pygmalion effect for raised performance would find more effectiveness of they were to be pleasant and friendly.  Liden and Maslyn (1998) reported, “It is widely agreed that positive affective feelings are part of a high-quality LMX relationship (Liden & Maslyn, 1998, p. 43). 
What has often been found in the Pygmalion literature is that the LMX relationship must be high for Pygmalion to take effect. What is not often discussed however is how trust is vital in this relationship. There should be more studies on this element of LMX and Pygmalion. Karakowski (2012) expressed that “the link between leader support and subordinate self-expectations remains largely a black box with no real explanation for the conditions under which leader expectations and support can effectively trigger the Pygmalion effect”(Karakowski, 2012, p. 580). Trust is a complicated aspect to identify and define because there are many different types and levels of trust. More research and studies in this area should help Pygmalion to be more useful in the business setting.
Theoretical Framework
As the above review supports, trust is the repeated element seen in the Pygmalion Effect. These trust elements may be hard to determine as to what levels and in what forms they become the most effective catalyst to the Pygmalion Effect. To support the literature review, the study initiated was done with the primary factor of trust being the undertone. A survey (Appendix A) was designed and administered. The survey uses a convenient sample with CH2M Hill and involves 300 people, 70 of who chose to take the survey. This group is all business professionals who varied in rank. The survey was anonymous, optional and measured with a Likert scale. A survey invitation was sent via email from our client Marie Gettel to three-hundred of her co-workers. The email directed respondents to a Survey Monkey link which was where all of the data was stored and retrieved from.  The survey attempts to see if there are correlations in five factors: 
•	Trust
•	Expectations
•	Performance 
•	Capability
•	Gender
In an effort to understand what kinds of trust are relevant, respondents were also asked to choose from a statement that describes their professional relationship with their supervisors (question #5) and simply how much they trust their supervisors (question #6). A goal of the survey is to see if personal trust in the supervisor correlates with employee performance. Another specific goal of the survey was to see if gender plays a role in these outcomes and the data was filtered for male and female responses as well as for males with female supervisors and males with male supervisors and the same for the female responses.
Discussion of Research
. The survey administered was designed to find possible correlations between the way respondents answered questions regarding the way they see themselves as meeting their supervisor’s expectations and how the respondents view these expectations in terms of being realistic or not. The survey looked at respondents’ views of their own performance and capabilities and compared these with what they think their supervisors’ views are on performance and capability. Special attention was given to questions regarding trust in an attemptto gauge the level of trust, what types of supervisor behaviors may represent trust and how this trust may effect subordinate performance. The level of expectations of one self, by self and supervisor, and whether the respondents feel they have met those expectations are attempted to be understood by this survey.
The end of the survey asked about gender which seems to play an important role in these relationships and the data for some correlations is skewed towards the male/female subordinate and male/female supervisor relationships. This sheds light on some interesting data of which inferences may be made, but also suggests that much more study could be done in these areas. Finally, it is worth noting that because this survey was administered to a small group of people (four hundred) all employed at the same company, there may be some biases or data skewness that is unseen. Overall this appears to be a relatively happy group who value and trust their supervisors at a high level. This of course is not the case for all companies, so the results must be observed with these considerations.
Analysis of Data
The data retrieved form the survey was imported as an Excel table (Appendix B), transferred to Mini-Tab for analysis and from there it was analyzed by using correlation testing. Pearson correlation and P-Values were looked at for the analysis and for those with statistical importance; the P-Values of the highly correlated data are included in this report (Appendix C).
Results from the data support that trust levels of employees in their supervisors and the perceived meeting of supervisor’s expectations do indeed correlate. When asked if they met their supervisors expectations there was a strong correlation with whether or not they trust their supervisors personally (P-Value = 0.033). The respondents who described their supervisor as “friendly and caring” also had strong correlation between meeting their own and their supervisors expectations (P-Value = 0.021). 
In terms of gender, one of the correlations which may need more study to understand fully is that the high correlation between trust and a supervisor’s perceived understanding of one’s capabilities were seen in all categories except in males with female supervisors which had a P-Value of 0.084 (well above the target of a 0.05 P-Value for strong correlation). A P-Value worth mentioning is that females with female supervisors showed a strong correlation between meeting their own expectations at work with thinking they are capable of doing more than is expected of them (P-Value = 0.029).
When it comes to feeling that respondents meet their own expectations correlating with whether or not they think they should perform better, I found data I thought was very interesting. There was a high correlation for the three of the four groups (males with male supervisors, females with male and female supervisors)but no correlation for males with female supervisors.
The correlation for meeting one’s own expectations and thinking that building trust with one’s supervisor involves getting to know them personally was drastically different for two of these groups. There was a high correlation for males with male supervisors and no correlation for females with male supervisors.
Conclusion/Recommendations 
The data received from the study shows that trust is a significant factor in an employee’s self-perceived performance. Finally, it is worth noting that because this survey was administered to a small group of people all employed at the same company, there may be some biases or data skewness that is unseen. Overall this appears to be a relatively happy group who value and trust their supervisors at a high level. This of course is not the case for all companies, so the results must be observed with these considerations. 
Some inferences that can be made are that getting to know a subordinate personally generally coincides with trust and in turn higher meeting of supervisors’ expectations. Of course it is not difficult to understand that people work harder for supervisors that are seen as friendly and caring and the literature reviewed and data retrieved form the survey supported this.
 In terms of gender and the one category mentioned, male with female supervisors, where trust and the supervisor’s ability to understand the subordinates capabilities do not correlate where other groups did, more study would be prudent. This result may be because the males surveyed do not think that their female supervisors understand them, or that whether they understand them or not is insignificant to their level of trust, or simply that they have some sexist beliefs affecting all of these factors. This example makes clear that there is not only a need for more studies of trust in the workplace, but also that the data may reflect many different possibilities and again, it would not be prudent to make assumptions about the data especially when it is based on feelings. Emotions are hard to gauge and people’s ideas about feelings are subjective and not scientific.
What can be said, that is not a new idea, is that treating subordinates well enforces better performance. What may be a new idea to some, however, is that understanding a subordinates jobs and capabilities, and getting to know them personally and showing kindness may raise the subordinates trust in the supervisor and make them more likely to meet the supervisor’s raised expectations. What has yet to be mentioned about the data so far is that the majority of all the respondents (62 out of 70) all believe that they are capable of doing more than is expected of them. Seventy percent of respondents also disagreed that their supervisor expects more from them than they can accomplish. These are two of the most encouraging statistics for these supervisors in that it tells them that raising expectations is not necessarily unreasonable. Now just because someone is capable of doing more does not mean that they should, but that is where trust and the Pygmalion relationship come into play. 














